    "title": "Veteran Salute: Paxico veteran learns valuable lessons to last a lifetime after military service - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Savannah Meier ,\n1 hours ago\nPAXICO ( KSNT ) – A Paxico man found himself drafted to the Army at the height of the Vietnam War. The now decorated veteran said his time in the service helped him grow as a person.\nDavid Berringer was drafted into the Army in 1968. At age 23, he was the oldest man in his training battalion. But this did not stop him from taking it as a learning opportunity.\n“It was at a time when I got drafted, which was not a comfortable time for me,” Berringer said. “I didn’t really know where I was going, didn’t really know what I was doing. And so, when I went in, I learned that, obviously, being the oldest guy drafted at that moment, I learned how to lead. I learned how to listen to orders, and I learned how to differentiate between good things that were happening around me, bad things that were happening around me, and still come out on the other end.”\nVeteran Salute: The Navy mindset continues on after two decades of service In his time in the Army, Berringer held many jobs: from being a battery clerk filling out reports to a gun sergeant. He said one lesson he took home is that everything is a team effort.\nHowever, his return stateside wasn’t quite what he was expecting.\n“When I came home in 1970, the military was not really received well when it came back because of Vietnam,” Berringer said. “So that’s a moment that was depressing to me. However, as it grew through the years, I became very proud of my service. I’m glad I did it. I’m glad I went. I did what my country asked me to do. And today, I fly the American flag. I fly the P.O.W. Flag in my den. And I’m just happy that I served. And I know that today people treat you with respect.”\nBerringer worked for Tenneco Oil for two decades, eventually settling down in Paxico with his wife. Though now 20 years retired, Berringer never misses a day working the Paxico antique store, where he gets to meet people from all over the world and hear their stories.\nIf you know a veteran you would like to nominate and showcase, email veterans@ksnt.com.\nFor more Veteran Salute, click here . Keep up with the latest breaking news in northeast Kansas by downloading our mobile app and by signing up for our news email alerts . Sign up for our Storm Track Weather app by clicking here .\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KSNT 27 News.",
